
# EB-1A SCHOLARLY ARTICLES CRITERION: EXPERT EXAMINER GUIDE & GOLD-STANDARD TEMPLATE

## PART 1: WHAT EXPERT USCIS EXAMINERS LOOK FOR

### Regulatory Standard (8 C.F.R. § 204.5(h)(3)(vi))
"Evidence of the alien's authorship of scholarly articles in the field, in professional or major trade publications or other major media."

### A. TWO-STEP KAZARIAN ANALYSIS FRAMEWORK

#### STEP 1: Plain Language Review
Examiners verify if submitted evidence literally meets the regulatory criterion:

*1. AUTHORSHIP VERIFICATION*
•⁠  ⁠*What I Look For:*
  - Complete publication with ALL pages (title page, author list, abstract, full text, references)
  - Clear author attribution showing beneficiary's name exactly as claimed
  - Author affiliation matching beneficiary's credentials
  - Publication date and venue clearly visible
  
•⁠  ⁠*Common Failures I See:*
  - Incomplete articles (missing title pages or methodology sections)
  - Preprints without proof of final publication in peer-reviewed venue
  - Conference abstracts submitted as "articles" (abstracts ≠ articles)
  - Blog posts or non-peer-reviewed content
  - Working papers never formally published
  - Author name discrepancies (different spellings, missing middle initials)

•⁠  ⁠*Gold Standard Evidence:*
  - Complete PDF of published article with journal header/footer
  - Screenshot from journal website showing article in issue table of contents
  - For online journals: persistent DOI and URL
  - For foreign language: certified English translation of ENTIRE article (not just abstract)

*2. SCHOLARLY NATURE ASSESSMENT*
What constitutes "scholarly" per USCIS Policy Manual (Vol. 6, Part F, Ch. 2):

•⁠  ⁠*Must Report:*
  - Original research with methodology
  - Experimental results and analysis
  - Philosophical discourse with rigorous argumentation
  - Technical innovation with validation

•⁠  ⁠*Does NOT Qualify as Scholarly:*
  - News articles or press releases about research
  - Popular science articles in non-peer-reviewed outlets
  - Technical blog posts (even on Medium or company blogs)
  - White papers without peer review
  - Conference presentation slides
  - Posters or extended abstracts
  - Opinion pieces or editorials without original research
  - Book reviews or literature surveys alone

•⁠  ⁠*Critical Test I Apply:*
  "Would this be cited in academic literature as a primary research source?"

*3. PUBLICATION VENUE STANDARDS*

Examiners assess whether venue qualifies as "professional or major trade publications or other major media":

*For AI/Data Science/Security Fields, I Accept:*

*Peer-Reviewed Journals:*
•⁠  ⁠IEEE Transactions series (TPAMI, TKDE, TNNLS, etc.)
•⁠  ⁠ACM journals (TODS, TOIS, TOCHI, etc.)
•⁠  ⁠Nature/Science family journals
•⁠  ⁠Field-specific journals: Machine Learning, JMLR, Neural Computation
•⁠  ⁠Cybersecurity journals: IEEE Security & Privacy, Computers & Security

*Top-Tier Conference Proceedings (with full peer review):*
•⁠  ⁠NeurIPS, ICML, ICLR, CVPR, ICCV, ECCV
•⁠  ⁠ACL, EMNLP, NAACL (for NLP)
•⁠  ⁠USENIX Security, IEEE S&P, CCS, NDSS (for security)
•⁠  ⁠KDD, SIGMOD, VLDB (for data science)
•⁠  ⁠AAAI, IJCAI (for AI)

*What I Need to See for Venue Verification:*
•⁠  ⁠Evidence of peer review process (from journal/conference website)
•⁠  ⁠Impact factor or h-index for journals
•⁠  ⁠Acceptance rate for conferences (especially if <25%)
•⁠  ⁠Ranking in field (CORE rankings, Google Scholar metrics)
•⁠  ⁠Editorial board credentials
•⁠  ⁠Indexing in major databases (PubMed, IEEE Xplore, ACM Digital Library, Web of Science, Scopus)

*Red Flags I Watch For:*
•⁠  ⁠Predatory journals (checked against Beall's List)
•⁠  ⁠Pay-to-publish venues without rigorous review
•⁠  ⁠Conferences with >50% acceptance rates
•⁠  ⁠Vanity publishers
•⁠  ⁠Self-published proceedings
•⁠  ⁠Journals not indexed in major databases
•⁠  ⁠New journals without established reputation (<3 years old without strong editorial board)

*4. AUTHOR CONTRIBUTION VERIFICATION*

*For Multi-Author Papers, I Assess:*
•⁠  ⁠Author position (first, corresponding, or senior author carries more weight)
•⁠  ⁠Evidence of beneficiary's specific contribution
•⁠  ⁠For large collaborations: documentation showing beneficiary's role wasn't merely administrative

*What Strengthens the Case:*
•⁠  ⁠First author or corresponding author designation
•⁠  ⁠Letters from co-authors detailing beneficiary's contributions
•⁠  ⁠For large teams: evidence beneficiary led specific component
•⁠  ⁠Equal contribution statements (common in AI/ML)

#### STEP 2: Final Merits Determination

*Even if publications meet Step 1, I evaluate:*

*A. QUANTITY vs. QUALITY ANALYSIS*

*Raw Numbers Alone Are Insufficient:*
•⁠  ⁠5 publications in top venues > 50 in predatory journals
•⁠  ⁠3 highly-cited papers > 30 with zero citations

*What I Look For in AI/Data Science:*
•⁠  ⁠*Emerging researcher (2-5 years post-PhD):* 4-8 strong publications
•⁠  ⁠*Mid-career (5-10 years):* 10-20+ publications with citation impact
•⁠  ⁠*Senior researcher:* Extensive publication record with demonstrated influence

*B. IMPACT AND RECOGNITION INDICATORS*

*Citation Analysis (CRITICAL for AI/Data Science):*
•⁠  ⁠Total citations across all papers (I check Google Scholar, Web of Science)
•⁠  ⁠H-index appropriate for career stage
•⁠  ⁠Citation trajectory (increasing vs. declining)
•⁠  ⁠Citations from independent researchers (not self-citations or from close collaborators)
•⁠  ⁠Geographic diversity of citations (international vs. single country)
•⁠  ⁠Citations in subsequent survey papers or textbooks

*Benchmarks I Use for AI/Data Science:*
•⁠  ⁠*Strong case:* 500+ citations, h-index 10+
•⁠  ⁠*Very strong:* 1,000+ citations, h-index 15+
•⁠  ⁠*Exceptional:* 2,000+ citations, h-index 20+

*But Context Matters:*
•⁠  ⁠Subfield norms (computer vision vs. theoretical ML)
•⁠  ⁠Career stage
•⁠  ⁠Publication recency

*C. SYNERGY WITH OTHER CRITERIA*

*I Look for Evidence That Publications Led To:*

1.⁠ ⁠*Peer Review Invitations:* 
   - Invited to review for top conferences/journals
   - Evidence: reviewer certificates, editorial board membership

2.⁠ ⁠*Published Material About Beneficiary:*
   - Media coverage of research findings
   - Profile features in IEEE Spectrum, MIT Technology Review, etc.
   - Citations in policy documents or industry reports

3.⁠ ⁠*Original Contributions:*
   - Patents based on publications
   - Adoption of methods by other researchers
   - Implementation in major software libraries (TensorFlow, PyTorch, etc.)

4.⁠ ⁠*Judging/Review:*
   - Serving as program committee member for major conferences
   - Editorial board positions

5.⁠ ⁠*Leading Role:*
   - Research leadership positions arising from publication record

