CRITICAL INSIGHTS FOR AI/DATA SCIENCE/SECURITY FIELD:
 1.⁠ ⁠Conference Papers Are Strong Evidence
In AI and computer science, peer-reviewed conference papers at top-tier venues (NeurIPS, ICML, CVPR, USENIX Security) with acceptance rates below 25% are considered scholarly articles of equal or greater prestige than journal publications INVENTIMM, PC. Make sure to document:

Acceptance rates
CORE rankings (aim for A* or A)
h5-index of the conference
Double-blind peer review process

 2.⁠ ⁠Citation Metrics That Matter
Based on successful AI/ML cases, strong profiles show 500-1,000+ citations with h-index of 10-15+ for emerging researchers, and 2,000+ citations with h-index 20+ for senior researchers WeGreened. However, quality trumps quantity:

Focus on independent citations
Highlight citations from leading researchers
Show geographic diversity (citations from multiple countries)
Demonstrate citation velocity (citations per year)

 3.⁠ ⁠Open Source Integration Strengthens Your Case
For AI/data science, examiners now recognize:

GitHub repository metrics (stars, forks, contributors)
Integration into major frameworks (TensorFlow, PyTorch, scikit-learn)
Package download statistics (pip installs, conda downloads)
This shows real-world impact beyond academic citations

 4.⁠ ⁠Common Pitfalls to Avoid
Based on recent AAO denials:
❌ Incomplete documentation: Literature that mentions someone's paper in passing is not sufficient; USCIS wants substantial discussion about the beneficiary Shautsova
❌ arXiv preprints without peer review: Must show eventual publication in peer-reviewed venue
❌ Workshop papers: Unless they're archival with full peer review
❌ Technical blog posts: Even on prestigious platforms like Medium or company blogs
❌ Missing venue prestige documentation: Don't assume USCIS knows your conference/journal is top-tier
 5.⁠ ⁠The Two-Step Kazarian Test
USCIS now applies a rigorous two-step analysis: first, determine whether evidence meets the plain text of each regulatory criterion; second, conduct a final merits review of the whole record at EB-1A's very high bar Curran, Berger & Kludt.
You must satisfy BOTH steps:

Step 1: Prove publications are scholarly, peer-reviewed, and in professional venues
Step 2: Prove these publications demonstrate you're at the "very top" of your field

 6.⁠ ⁠Synergy is Powerful
Examiners increasingly look for synergy among EB-1A evidence—where one criterion demonstrates or amplifies standing in others INVENTIMM, PC. Your publications should connect to:

Judging criterion: Reviewer invitations based on publication record
Original contributions: Publications documenting your innovations
Published material about you: Media coverage of your research
Leading role: Publications showing research leadership

 7.⁠ ⁠Expert Letters Must Be Specific
Don't let recommenders write generic praise. They should:

Cite specific papers and explain their significance
Provide quantitative context ("top 5% of researchers at this career stage")
Compare to field leaders ("comparable to work by [famous researcher]")
Explain real-world impact ("adopted by X companies/used in Y systems")

 8.⁠ ⁠Recent Success Factors for AI/Data Science Cases
From successful EB-1A approvals in AI/ML, strong cases typically showed: 4-10 peer-reviewed publications, 500-700+ citations, demonstrated real-world impact (NSF funding, industry adoption), specific contributions to advancing the field, and clear evidence of international recognition Success Stories: EB1-A Victory Made Possible for an Indian Data Scientist in Just 14 Days, Thanks to the Premium Processing Upgrade and Our Team’s Work | Chen Immigration Blog +2.
 9.⁠ ⁠Documentation Best Practices
For each publication, create a "publication packet":

Complete PDF of published article
One-page venue information sheet (acceptance rate, rankings, impact factor)
Citation report for that specific paper
2-3 highlighted "best citations" with context
